      <p class="eyebrow">About</p>
      <h1>Hi, I'm AJ.</h1>
      <p>
        I grew up on a farm raising pigs, cows, and chickens. When I sold the
        first animal I raised, my dad told me the proceeds were mine to put into
        any stock I chose. That was my introduction to business, and it is
        fairly directly why I went to business school.
      </p>
      <p>
        I studied at UC Berkeley's Haas School of Business, class of 2025, and
        was admitted to the Global Management Program, which sent me to London
        for a semester. It left me with a habit of looking at problems from a
        global angle and a durable interest in how other markets actually
        operate. I also took data science coursework, which is where I found the
        work I enjoy most: building something and watching it solve a real
        problem.
      </p>

      <h2>Consulting</h2>
      <p>
        I spent three years at Berkeley ABA Consulting, moving from analyst to
        project manager to senior advisor. The client work included IKEA,
        T-Mobile, and the Gates Foundation. For two semesters I was the project
        manager on the nonprofit team, running engagements for The Assistance
        Fund and the Gates Foundation.
      </p>
      <p>
        That nonprofit work is what I would point to if asked what I want my
        career to add up to. I would like the long arc of it to leave things
        better than I found them, and the way I think I can contribute is by
        building useful things and being honest about whether they work.
      </p>
      <p>
        I also completed two international internships, one in London and one
        with a Tokyo-based company that sent me to CES. Both were small enough
        that the work was real, and both are why ambiguity does not bother me
        much.
      </p>

      <h2>Now</h2>
      <p>
        I am an analyst at EY in San Francisco, in the financial services
        assurance practice, working across banking engagements.
      </p>
      <p>
        Outside of work I build, and nearly all of it now runs through AI
        tooling, Claude Code in particular. That includes this site,
        <a href="/projects/nottomorrow">NotTomorrow</a>, a
        <a href="/projects/deadlock-counter-buy">recommendation engine</a>, and
        <a href="/projects/scaffolding-vs-scale">an empirical study</a> of how
        AI models behave on real occupational work. The study taught me more
        about research than any coursework did, largely because it went wrong in
        an instructive way.
      </p>

      <h2>What I'm looking for</h2>
      <p>
        A full-time role where analytical rigor and shipping speed both matter:
        AI implementation and applied AI, product management, data and
        analytics, strategy and operations, or strategic finance. Anywhere in
        the Bay Area works, San Francisco through the Peninsula and South Bay,
        and I am open to Santa Barbara or remote.
      </p>

      <h2>Outside work</h2>
      <p>
        I am competitive, and most of what I do for fun reflects that:
        basketball, soccer, skiing, chess, and training seriously enough that
        fitness shapes how I structure a week. I follow the semiconductor and
        data-center industry closely, and I have a
        <a href="/how-i-work">standing AI workflow</a> that assembles a weekly
        economics digest for me. I also travel whenever I can, usually with an
        unreasonable amount of effort spent optimizing the trip.
      </p>

